I'll write the prompt now.
Write a self-contained Python 3 command-line script (intended to run under
Termux on Android, so use a `#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`
shebang and `from __future__ import annotations`) that reads a JSON file,
rewrites bracket characters inside every JSON string literal, and writes the
result back.
Use only the standard library: `json`, `re`, `sys`, and `pathlib.Path`.
**Purpose and usage** - Invoked as `<script> <json-file>` with exactly one
argument.
If the argument count is not exactly two, print a usage message of the form
`usage: <script name> <json-file>` (script name taken from the invoked path's
basename) and exit via `sys.exit` with that message. - The script processes a
single JSON file given by that path. **String detection** - Define a
module-level compiled regex matching a double-quoted JSON-style string: an
opening `"`, then any sequence of either a non-quote, non-backslash character or
a backslash followed by any character, then a closing `"`.
Use it with `re.sub` over the raw file text so that only string literals are
altered and structural JSON outside strings is left untouched. **Per-string
transformation (`fix_content`)** Process the inner content of each matched
string character by character with an index loop: - A backslash followed by `[`
or `{` is replaced with `(` (the backslash and the bracket are both consumed,
two characters advanced). - A backslash followed by `]` or `}` is replaced with
`)`. - A backslash followed by `(` or `)` is emitted as that parenthesis alone
(backslash dropped). - Any other backslash escape is copied through unchanged as
the backslash plus the following character. - A backslash at the very end of the
content with no following character is copied as-is. - An unescaped `[` or `{`
becomes `(`; an unescaped `]` or `}` becomes `)`; all other characters are
copied unchanged. - After the loop, count `(` and `)` in the resulting string.
If there are more opens than closes, append the missing `)` characters at the
end.
If there are more closes than opens, prepend the missing `(` characters at the
start.
Otherwise leave it unchanged. **Per-match wrapper (`fix_literal`)** - Takes a
regex match object, takes the full matched text, strips the surrounding quote
characters, applies `fix_content` to the inner text, and returns it re-wrapped
in double quotes. **`main` flow** - Validate argument count as above. - Convert
the argument to a `Path`.
Read the file as text (UTF-8).
Apply the string regex substitution using `fix_literal`. - Parse-check or write
the output so that the file is saved with the transformed text.
Ensure the result is written back to the same path (overwriting the original)
using UTF-8, and handle the case where the file is missing or unreadable by
printing an error and exiting non-zero rather than crashing with a traceback. -
Include a `if __name__ == "__main__": main()` guard. - Type-annotate functions
(`-> str`, `-> None`, `re.Match` parameter) consistent with `from __future__
import annotations`. **Edge cases and behavior notes** - Brackets outside string
literals are not modified. - Escaped quotes inside strings are handled by the
regex's backslash-pair alternative, so `\"` does not terminate a string. - Paren
balancing is per string literal, not globally, and only adds parentheses at the
ends of each string. - The script makes no attempt to validate the JSON after
transformation; output correctness depends on the input. - Keep code style plain
and idiomatic: short helper functions, index-based loops where shown, minimal
comments, no external dependencies. ---
